<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/development-piaget-to-today">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Developmental psychology studies <strong>how minds get built</strong> — from newborn reflexes
    to adult reasoning. The field's two founding geniuses disagreed productively: Piaget saw the
    child as a solo scientist constructing logic; Vygotsky saw an apprentice absorbing culture.
    Modern findings braid both with genetics, and show infants knowing far more than anyone
    guessed.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the theorists, the overturns, and what survived" widget="equationStep" config={devCfg} />

  <h2>What we now know about young minds</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Core knowledge is innate-ish:</strong> infants expect objects to persist, add small numbers approximately, and prefer helpers over hinderers (Hamlin's puppet studies) — building blocks before language.</li>
    <li><strong>Executive function develops late:</strong> impulse control, working memory and planning mature through adolescence; the prefrontal cortex keeps remodelling into the mid-20s. Teenage risk-taking is neurodevelopment, not character.</li>
    <li><strong>Language has a sensitive period:</strong> exposure before ~7 years predicts native-like mastery; after puberty, grammar accents stay. The critical-window evidence is strongest here.</li>
    <li><strong>Screen time debates need nuance:</strong> content, context and sleep displacement matter more than raw hours — the research consensus warns about panel averaging across very different exposures.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Piaget (1952); Vygotsky (1934); Baillargeon (1987); Hamlin et al., Nature (2007); Sroufe longitudinal attachment studies.</p>
</TopicPage>
